WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:03.402 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:03.402 --> 00:00:06.072
-Hey, everybody.
Welcome to "Corrections,"

00:00:06.072 --> 00:00:09.142
week of July 12th.

00:00:09.142 --> 00:00:11.711
I double-checked that
to make sure it was right,

00:00:11.711 --> 00:00:15.648
because last time we did this,
I opened by saying,

00:00:15.648 --> 00:00:20.587
"Welcome to 'Corrections,'
week of Monday, June 24th."

00:00:20.587 --> 00:00:23.222
It was Thursday, June 24th.

00:00:23.222 --> 00:00:25.791
And that is the fastest mistake
I've ever made

00:00:25.791 --> 00:00:28.762
in a "Corrections."

00:00:28.762 --> 00:00:31.097
I mean, that's embarrassing.

00:00:31.097 --> 00:00:34.234
That's like if the minute
the Kentucky Derby started,

00:00:34.234 --> 00:00:35.768
one of the horses exploded.

00:00:35.768 --> 00:00:37.703
[ Light laughter ]

00:00:37.703 --> 00:00:39.138
But we got it right.

00:00:39.138 --> 00:00:42.442
Also, you might have noticed
from the title card,

00:00:42.442 --> 00:00:48.147
this is now an Emmy-nominated
piece of television content.

00:00:48.147 --> 00:00:50.183
That is not a joke.

00:00:50.183 --> 00:00:53.586
It sounds like a joke.
It is not a joke.

00:00:53.586 --> 00:00:58.224
And this is also not a joke,
and I mean it sincerely --

00:00:58.224 --> 00:01:00.626
I could not have done this
without you.

00:01:00.626 --> 00:01:04.631
We share this honor
because I think you know

00:01:04.631 --> 00:01:08.634
that without you,
there's no "Corrections."

00:01:08.634 --> 00:01:11.837
This was a team effort.
And I also want you to know

00:01:11.837 --> 00:01:17.377
that if we should win,
all the rhetoric

00:01:17.377 --> 00:01:19.846
about it being a team effort --
right out the window.

00:01:19.846 --> 00:01:22.582
[ Laughter ]

00:01:22.582 --> 00:01:26.486
It is mine, and mine alone,

00:01:26.486 --> 00:01:29.856
and, oh, I'm gonna lord it
over you.

00:01:29.856 --> 00:01:31.824
Oh, you'll be giving me
your corrections,

00:01:31.824 --> 00:01:33.559
and I'll be like, "What?

00:01:33.559 --> 00:01:35.761
Finland doesn't have mountains?
I can't hear you...

00:01:35.761 --> 00:01:37.797
over my Emmy."

00:01:37.797 --> 00:01:41.468
[ Light laughter ]

00:01:41.468 --> 00:01:46.005
And if I lose, I will say
it was stolen.

00:01:46.005 --> 00:01:47.040
I'm getting the whole --

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:50.977
I'm getting Sidney Powell,
Giuliani.

00:01:50.977 --> 00:01:57.450
We will rain hell down
upon the Academy.

00:01:59.652 --> 00:02:05.024
It is, uh --
It's Outstanding Short Form

00:02:05.024 --> 00:02:07.993
Drama, Comedy, or Variety...

00:02:07.993 --> 00:02:11.097
which I feel like
we tick all those boxes.

00:02:11.097 --> 00:02:13.433
I feel like there's
a little of each in here.

00:02:15.935 --> 00:02:18.571
As with a lot of "Corrections,"
we have to clean up stuff

00:02:18.571 --> 00:02:20.473
from previous "Corrections."

00:02:20.473 --> 00:02:22.342
We've talked a lot about

00:02:22.342 --> 00:02:26.379
how -- or whether or not
roosters can blink or wink.

00:02:28.914 --> 00:02:30.817
I'm just realizing
that maybe people who

00:02:30.817 --> 00:02:32.752
had never watched "Corrections"
and are now tuning in

00:02:32.752 --> 00:02:37.257
because it's Emmy nominated
are like, "Duh fuh?!"

00:02:37.257 --> 00:02:39.559
But, yeah, no, roosters blinking

00:02:39.559 --> 00:02:44.196
has been, like,
a multi-episode arc.

00:02:44.196 --> 00:02:47.734
They have a membrane that wipes
the dust, like a clear membrane.

00:02:47.734 --> 00:02:50.336
I called it
a "nictating" membrane.

00:02:50.336 --> 00:02:54.473
It's<i> "nictitating."</i>
Nictitating.

00:02:54.473 --> 00:02:55.774
And I cannot tell you --

00:02:55.774 --> 00:02:57.176
You know,
sometimes there's one thing

00:02:57.176 --> 00:02:58.544
that maybe one of you catches.

00:02:58.544 --> 00:03:02.581
100 people
pointed out that I got it wrong.

00:03:02.581 --> 00:03:04.516
And obviously --
I don't need to tell you --

00:03:04.516 --> 00:03:05.785
I'd never heard of it,

00:03:05.785 --> 00:03:12.658
and yet so many of you
know about chicken's eyes,

00:03:12.658 --> 00:03:14.927
and this is a real shame-on-me.

00:03:14.927 --> 00:03:16.729
But, like, I think
I've always kind of, like,

00:03:16.729 --> 00:03:18.464
thought of our audience

00:03:18.464 --> 00:03:24.236
as metropolitan and urban,
and you're farm people.

00:03:24.236 --> 00:03:27.573
You know?
You're watching this.

00:03:27.573 --> 00:03:29.374
You got, like, an iPhone
in one hand,

00:03:29.374 --> 00:03:31.310
and you're just, like,
throwing -- The other hand's,

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:35.514
like, throwing seed
to your livestock.

00:03:35.514 --> 00:03:38.952
And I worry about
your livestock...

00:03:38.952 --> 00:03:41.154
because they live
with (bleep) jackals.

00:03:41.154 --> 00:03:43.322
[ Light laughter ]

00:03:44.456 --> 00:03:48.761
Bjork. This is -- Again, Bjork
has been famous for so long,

00:03:48.761 --> 00:03:52.331
and I was told by a lot
of people I said it wrong.

00:03:52.331 --> 00:03:54.934
And sometimes
when people tell me

00:03:54.934 --> 00:03:57.637
how to say it,
it's not helpful

00:03:57.637 --> 00:04:01.206
because I have to read
their pronunciation.

00:04:01.206 --> 00:04:04.977
And so I found a video
of an Icelandic --

00:04:04.977 --> 00:04:06.312
Icelandic, an Icelander --

00:04:06.312 --> 00:04:09.615
I don't -- There's probably
a word, and...

00:04:09.615 --> 00:04:11.183
I only wish we had
an infrastructure

00:04:11.183 --> 00:04:14.954
where you could tell me
what that word was.

00:04:14.954 --> 00:04:17.056
He said -- I watched the video
where he said --

00:04:17.056 --> 00:04:21.094
I think it's close to this.
Byerk.

00:04:21.094 --> 00:04:22.728
So I'm sure
that's not quite right,

00:04:22.728 --> 00:04:24.597
but it's definitely not
"B-york."

00:04:24.597 --> 00:04:29.235
And then we also said
when we mentioned it, that jork

00:04:29.235 --> 00:04:33.706
was an Icelandic word
for joke about Bjork.

00:04:33.706 --> 00:04:36.108
And then someone said, "Well,

00:04:36.108 --> 00:04:40.312
if the "J" in "Bjork"
is silence -- silent,

00:04:40.312 --> 00:04:43.750
it wouldn't be jork.
It would be "york."

00:04:43.750 --> 00:04:46.785
Sure.

00:04:46.785 --> 00:04:50.523
But you're being a "yerk."

00:04:50.523 --> 00:04:52.525
[ Man laughs ]

00:04:55.861 --> 00:05:00.232
The hardest part about this
is that, you know,

00:05:00.232 --> 00:05:02.201
you got to read all the
comments. You got to mine

00:05:02.201 --> 00:05:05.671
the comments
to find the corrections.

00:05:05.671 --> 00:05:08.741
And sometimes you're
gonna find some people

00:05:08.741 --> 00:05:12.077
who maybe just
don't like the show.

00:05:12.077 --> 00:05:16.282
But there was an exchange that
I really greatly enjoyed,

00:05:16.282 --> 00:05:17.984
which is someone --
And by the way,

00:05:17.984 --> 00:05:21.387
I celebrate that this show
is not for everybody.

00:05:21.387 --> 00:05:22.921
Someone wrote,
"This guy is so bad,

00:05:22.921 --> 00:05:27.727
the audience doesn't want
to laugh at his jokes."

00:05:27.727 --> 00:05:31.664
And then very kindly, someone
responded to that and said,

00:05:31.664 --> 00:05:35.301
"He doesn't have an audience
at the moment."

00:05:35.301 --> 00:05:37.069
And then the first person
wrote back,

00:05:37.069 --> 00:05:39.005
"That's a sad excuse."

00:05:39.005 --> 00:05:41.407
[ Laughter ]

00:05:41.407 --> 00:05:45.077
Like, it might be --
It might be sad

00:05:45.077 --> 00:05:50.682
that we don't have an audience,
but it's an airtight excuse

00:05:50.682 --> 00:05:53.386
for why people aren't laughing.

00:05:55.421 --> 00:05:57.557
"Why didn't Kevin come
to my birthday party?"

00:05:57.557 --> 00:05:59.959
"He's dead."
"Okay."

00:05:59.959 --> 00:06:01.828
[ Laughter ]

00:06:02.961 --> 00:06:05.564
[ Laughing ]
Can you imagine?

00:06:05.564 --> 00:06:07.433
Can you imagine?
Look, I get that

00:06:07.433 --> 00:06:09.168
maybe you think, like,
these jokes aren't funny.

00:06:09.168 --> 00:06:11.603
Do you really think I'm doing it

00:06:11.603 --> 00:06:14.707
for 180 people right now

00:06:14.707 --> 00:06:16.542
and they're all just like, "Oh"?

00:06:16.542 --> 00:06:19.045
[ Laughter ]

00:06:19.045 --> 00:06:22.381
What? This -- This would be
the funniest show

00:06:22.381 --> 00:06:23.749
in the world
to be watching right now

00:06:23.749 --> 00:06:26.652
if there were 180 people.

00:06:26.652 --> 00:06:28.054
I admit it's Monday.

00:06:28.054 --> 00:06:29.755
We don't have audiences back
until the f--

00:06:29.755 --> 00:06:31.690
On Monday, I'm gonna be like,
"We're so happy.

00:06:31.690 --> 00:06:34.560
Our audience is back," and then
I'm just gonna do the show.

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:36.929
[ Laughs ]

00:06:36.929 --> 00:06:40.333
What would the warm-up comedian
have to say?

00:06:42.968 --> 00:06:45.404
Is it like
the bus from "Speed" rules?

00:06:45.404 --> 00:06:50.576
Like, if we go under 50
or one of you makes a sound...

00:06:54.646 --> 00:06:57.082
We've definitely learned if
we're gonna make a mistake,

00:06:57.082 --> 00:06:59.251
don't make it in writing.

00:06:59.251 --> 00:07:02.020
Like, I can sometimes make
a flub and get it through,

00:07:02.020 --> 00:07:06.292
but if we make a mistake
in a graphic,

00:07:06.292 --> 00:07:08.927
you guys go over it like
it's the Zapruder film.

00:07:08.927 --> 00:07:10.563
Don't take the bait.

00:07:13.766 --> 00:07:16.969
So we had a driver's license
of a cockroach.

00:07:16.969 --> 00:07:20.539
And the birthday
was August 18, 2019,

00:07:20.539 --> 00:07:24.243
and someone said, "Cockroaches
can't live longer than a year.

00:07:24.243 --> 00:07:25.811
That cockroach would be dead."

00:07:25.811 --> 00:07:29.414
And I'm not gonna argue
with the science of that.

00:07:29.414 --> 00:07:31.517
Anybody who is bold enough
to write that in "Corrections"

00:07:31.517 --> 00:07:32.785
obviously knows
what they're talking about

00:07:32.785 --> 00:07:34.286
when it comes to
the life-span of cockroaches.

00:07:34.286 --> 00:07:38.624
I do think they live longer
in New York.

00:07:38.624 --> 00:07:41.927
You know? I had a...

00:07:41.927 --> 00:07:46.465
This year in my kitchen,
I saw a cockroach

00:07:46.465 --> 00:07:49.568
that was wearing a hat
that said, "Vote Dinkins."

00:07:49.568 --> 00:07:50.870
[ Light laughter ]

00:07:55.374 --> 00:08:01.046
We also -- Then we had another
driver's license of a cicada,

00:08:01.046 --> 00:08:05.384
and someone pointed out
that the cicada's --

00:08:05.384 --> 00:08:09.955
The height of the cicada
was 1'11",

00:08:09.955 --> 00:08:15.661
and it weighed 2 ounces.

00:08:15.661 --> 00:08:22.768
And they said,
"Why was the height in imperial

00:08:22.768 --> 00:08:26.471
and the weight was in metric?"

00:08:26.471 --> 00:08:28.240
And all I know is,
we're gonna find out,

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:31.043
and heads are gonna roll,

00:08:31.043 --> 00:08:34.480
because that's one of
the hard, fast rules here --

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:38.017
not to mix those up.

00:08:38.017 --> 00:08:41.620
We had a...

00:08:41.620 --> 00:08:45.724
a key photo of me getting
an appendectomy.

00:08:45.724 --> 00:08:48.027
Can we look at that real quick?

00:08:48.027 --> 00:08:50.696
[ Laughter ]

00:08:50.696 --> 00:08:52.230
Again, so many people said

00:08:52.230 --> 00:08:54.600
that's definitely not where
that incision would be.

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:58.236
I'd say we got, like, 100
of those.

00:08:58.236 --> 00:09:00.606
And then one guy said,

00:09:00.606 --> 00:09:03.609
"CDC rules.
You'd be wearing a mask."

00:09:07.379 --> 00:09:10.650
All right, we talked about --
We said Alice Cooper...

00:09:12.785 --> 00:09:15.320
...bit the head off a chicken.

00:09:15.320 --> 00:09:18.023
And someone
came at us pretty hard

00:09:18.023 --> 00:09:20.259
and said we had conflated
two things.

00:09:20.259 --> 00:09:23.595
Ozzy Osbourne
bit the head off a bat.

00:09:23.595 --> 00:09:26.365
Alice Cooper threw a chicken
into the crowd,

00:09:26.365 --> 00:09:29.135
and the crowd
tore the chicken apart.

00:09:29.135 --> 00:09:32.204
And once again,
I'm fairly confident

00:09:32.204 --> 00:09:34.373
that was written by a stepdad.

00:09:35.374 --> 00:09:37.510
[ Light laughter ]

00:09:40.412 --> 00:09:42.981
"Alice Cooper bit
the head off a chicken."

00:09:42.981 --> 00:09:46.686
"Uh, guys,

00:09:46.686 --> 00:09:48.587
that is absolutely not the case.

00:09:48.587 --> 00:09:50.956
Dave, get out of here!

00:09:50.956 --> 00:09:52.624
Just don't want you
to embarrass yourself.

00:09:52.624 --> 00:09:55.928
You're embarrassing me in
front of my friends right now!

00:09:58.130 --> 00:10:00.766
Ozzy bit the head off a bat.

00:10:00.766 --> 00:10:02.834
Ozzy's got to live that down.

00:10:02.834 --> 00:10:06.305
Cooper didn't, man.

00:10:06.305 --> 00:10:08.073
Cooper threw a chicken
into the crowd,

00:10:08.073 --> 00:10:09.675
they tore it apart.

00:10:09.675 --> 00:10:11.410
That's all that happened."

00:10:11.410 --> 00:10:12.944
Why do I care?

00:10:12.944 --> 00:10:19.284
"'Cause you're gonna get sued
for libel if Alice Cooper

00:10:19.284 --> 00:10:20.353
hears you saying that.

00:10:20.353 --> 00:10:24.490
It's slander if you say it.

00:10:27.726 --> 00:10:29.628
"You seen
the new 'Modern Drummer'?

00:10:29.628 --> 00:10:31.564
[ Laughter ]

00:10:35.201 --> 00:10:37.503
Leiderman's on the cover.
Pretty cool."

00:10:37.503 --> 00:10:39.238
[ Laughter ]

00:10:42.407 --> 00:10:44.443
[ Laughter continues ]

00:10:44.443 --> 00:10:46.278
"It was a big deal, dude."

00:10:46.278 --> 00:10:48.547
[ Laughter ]

00:10:50.816 --> 00:10:55.554
We said that the C.E.O. of
Subway stands behind his tuna,

00:10:55.554 --> 00:10:58.257
and then we said
he stays downwind.

00:10:58.257 --> 00:11:01.226
We should have said upwind.
We heard that a lot.

00:11:01.226 --> 00:11:02.694
You know, the premise
being you wouldn't --

00:11:02.694 --> 00:11:05.764
you wouldn't want to smell it.

00:11:05.764 --> 00:11:07.265
Ooh, we said that --

00:11:07.265 --> 00:11:11.703
I guess fish now can get
addicted to stuff

00:11:11.703 --> 00:11:13.972
in the water like crystal meth,

00:11:13.972 --> 00:11:16.676
and then we showed a picture
of a shark with no teeth.

00:11:16.676 --> 00:11:19.044
And then I said --
It wasn't written,

00:11:19.044 --> 00:11:22.480
but I said, "Oh, I know.
A shark's not a fish,"

00:11:22.480 --> 00:11:24.116
worried that I would be
corrected

00:11:24.116 --> 00:11:26.785
because that's how in my head
you have me.

00:11:26.785 --> 00:11:30.422
And then a lot of you said,
"No, a shark is a fish."

00:11:30.422 --> 00:11:32.291
But you -- Yeah, but
you could hear yourselves

00:11:32.291 --> 00:11:34.593
saying that, though, right?

00:11:34.593 --> 00:11:36.095
But then this.

00:11:36.095 --> 00:11:37.630
I wanted to print this out
and get this exactly right,

00:11:37.630 --> 00:11:40.632
because this is something
somebody wrote

00:11:40.632 --> 00:11:43.569
about this question about,
is a shark a fish?

00:11:43.569 --> 00:11:46.138
It said,
"Not a correction per se,

00:11:46.138 --> 00:11:48.373
but a fun fact about fish.

00:11:48.373 --> 00:11:52.144
Fish are not a taxonomic group
the way vertebrates or mammals

00:11:52.144 --> 00:11:54.814
are because the group
does not include

00:11:54.814 --> 00:11:57.316
all the descendants
of a common ancestor.

00:11:57.316 --> 00:11:59.751
If it did, all reptiles,
amphibians, birds, and mammals

00:11:59.751 --> 00:12:03.689
would also be considered fish
because all are descended

00:12:03.689 --> 00:12:08.260
from an ancient lobe-finned fish
called Sarcopterygii.

00:12:08.260 --> 00:12:10.262
Fish is just
a term of convenience

00:12:10.262 --> 00:12:15.034
to refer to a diverse group
of swimming aquatic organisms."

00:12:15.034 --> 00:12:19.938
And not to correct a corrector,
but I...

00:12:19.938 --> 00:12:22.841
don't think that's a fun fact.

00:12:22.841 --> 00:12:24.076
[ Laughter ]

00:12:35.554 --> 00:12:41.560
We talked this week about how...

00:12:41.560 --> 00:12:47.065
Monday was the 20th anniversary
of when myself and Amy Poehler

00:12:47.065 --> 00:12:50.736
auditioned to be on "SNL,"
and so it is now --

00:12:50.736 --> 00:12:53.672
I'm coming up on 20 years
in the building,

00:12:53.672 --> 00:12:56.741
which is -- which is surreal.

00:12:56.741 --> 00:12:59.378
I have the same I.D.
It is...

00:12:59.378 --> 00:13:02.514
I am a baby.

00:13:02.514 --> 00:13:05.818
When you look at my first I.D.,
just, I'm a kid.

00:13:05.818 --> 00:13:07.186
[ Laughter ]

00:13:11.990 --> 00:13:13.725
First --
The first thing Loren said

00:13:13.725 --> 00:13:17.562
is, "We're gonna
get your teeth fixed."

00:13:17.562 --> 00:13:19.831
This is --
This is really exciting.

00:13:19.831 --> 00:13:23.636
We have a prop budget now.

00:13:23.636 --> 00:13:25.037
We got Emmy nominated,
and they said,

00:13:25.037 --> 00:13:28.274
"You can do props
in 'Corrections.'"

00:13:30.141 --> 00:13:31.443
I said,
"We have a props budget?"

00:13:31.443 --> 00:13:32.945
They said, "Prop."

00:13:32.945 --> 00:13:35.214
[ Laughter ]

00:13:35.214 --> 00:13:37.716
And I said, "Can I keep this?"
And they're like, "No."

00:13:37.716 --> 00:13:39.418
I've got to give it back.
And I'm like, "What?"

00:13:39.418 --> 00:13:41.487
They said,
"We're gonna melt it down."

00:13:44.456 --> 00:13:49.795
Last night -- So 20 years ago,
I met Mike Shoemaker,

00:13:49.795 --> 00:13:53.732
and obviously we've talked
a lot about our friendship.

00:13:53.732 --> 00:13:57.035
And last night
he and I went out to dinner

00:13:57.035 --> 00:14:00.773
and had a very nice night

00:14:00.773 --> 00:14:03.509
talking about
how lucky we've been, and...

00:14:07.545 --> 00:14:08.847
...I make fun of Mike.

00:14:08.847 --> 00:14:12.884
I'm not gonna do the voice today
because,

00:14:12.884 --> 00:14:16.989
you know, the category we're
nominated for is also drama.

00:14:20.625 --> 00:14:24.696
And we -- Mike Shoemaker
is the gateway

00:14:24.696 --> 00:14:27.599
to everything good
I have known in show business.

00:14:27.599 --> 00:14:30.402
And we rewarded ourselves
last night.

00:14:30.402 --> 00:14:32.171
We don't usually go out
to fancy dinners,

00:14:32.171 --> 00:14:35.040
but we went
to Eleven Madison Park.

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:39.244
It was a very fancy,
incredible meal.

00:14:39.244 --> 00:14:43.248
And when it was over, he said,
"Why do you think I --"

00:14:43.248 --> 00:14:46.151
because he had suggested it.
"Why do you think I wanted

00:14:46.151 --> 00:14:48.854
to go to Eleven Madison Park?"
And I said, "I don't know."

00:14:48.854 --> 00:14:50.856
He goes, "The eleven's
the important thing."

00:14:50.856 --> 00:14:53.726
And I said, "Is it because...

00:14:56.662 --> 00:15:00.865
...our friendship is an 11
out of 10?"

00:15:00.865 --> 00:15:04.737
And he said no, and then
he just slowly did this.

00:15:04.737 --> 00:15:07.306
[ Laughter ]

00:15:09.708 --> 00:15:13.012
And just, like, held it there
a really long time.

00:15:16.248 --> 00:15:21.453
Drama, comedy, variety.

00:15:21.453 --> 00:15:22.520
All right, you guys.

00:15:22.520 --> 00:15:24.489
Well,
I think we've done it again,

00:15:24.489 --> 00:15:26.658
and we will be back next week.

00:15:26.658 --> 00:15:28.894
This was "Corrections."

